msgid "JAX-RS providers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Although RESTEasy Reactive provides the same spec compliant behavior as "
"RESTEasy Classic does, it does not include the same exact provider "
"implementations at runtime."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The most common case where the difference in providers might result in "
"different behavior, is the included `javax.ws.rs.ext.ExceptionMapper` "
"implementations. To see what classes are included in the application, launch "
"the application in dev mode and navigate to http://localhost:8080/q/dev/io."
"quarkus.quarkus-resteasy-reactive/exception-mappers."
msgstr ""

msgid "Service Loading"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"RESTEasy Classic supports determining providers at build time using Java's "
"Service Loader. In order to ensure that all providers are determined at "
"build time,\n"
"RESTEasy Reactive does not support this feature. Instead, users that have "
"providers in application dependencies are encouraged to index those "
"dependencies\n"
"using one of the methods described in the xref:cdi-reference."
"adoc#bean_discovery[Bean Discovery] section of the CDI guide."
msgstr ""

msgid "Multipart support"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"HTTP Multipart support in RESTEasy Reactive does **not** reuse the same "
"types or annotations as RESTEasy Classic and thus users are encouraged to "
"read <<resteasy-reactive#multipart,this>> part of the reference "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

msgid "Default media types"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus uses smart defaults when determining the media type of JAX-RS "
"methods in order to simplify common use cases.\n"
"The difference between `quarkus-resteasy-reactive` and `quarkus-resteasy` is "
"the use of `text/plain` as the default media type instead of `text/html`\n"
"when the method returns a `String`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Client"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Reactive REST Client (`quarkus-rest-client-reactive` and its "
"dependencies) replace the legacy `quarkus-rest-client` but leverage Quarkus' "
"build time processing\n"
"and the unified I/O model provided by Vert.x."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following table matches the legacy REST Client dependencies with the new "
"Reactive REST Client ones."
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-reactive`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-jackson`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-reactive-jackson`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-jsonb`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-reactive-jsonb`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-jaxb`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-rest-client-reactive-jaxb`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Keycloak admin client"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using `quarkus-rest-client`, users can use the `quarkus-keycloak-admin-"
"client` to administer the target Keycloak instance\n"
"by leveraging the rest client."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using `quarkus-rest-client-reactive` however, users must use `quarkus-"
"keycloak-admin-client-reactive` to access the same functionality\n"
"and use the reactive REST Client."
msgstr ""

msgid "OIDC"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using `quarkus-rest-client`, users can use the `quarkus-oidc-client-"
"filter` extensions to acquire and refresh access tokens from OpenID Connect "
"and OAuth 2.0 compliant Authorization Servers."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using `quarkus-rest-client-reactive` however, users must use `quarkus-"
"oidc-client-filter-reactive` to access the same functionality."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Similarly, `quarkus-oidc-token-propagation` allows user of the legacy REST "
"to propagate the current `Bearer` or `Authorization Code Flow` access tokens."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using `quarkus-rest-client-reactive` however, users must use `quarkus-"
"oidc-token-propagation-reactive` to access the same functionality."
msgstr ""

msgid "Custom extensions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is an advanced section that only needs to be read by users who have "
"developed custom extensions that depend on JAX-RS and / or REST Client "
"functionality."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A first concern is whether custom extensions should depend on RESTEasy "
"Reactive explicitly, or alternatively support both RESTEasy flavors and "
"leave it to the user to decide.\n"
"If the extension is some general purpose extension, it probably makes sense "
"to choose the latter option, while the former option is easiest to adopt "
"when the custom\n"
"extension is used by a specific set of users / applications."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When opting for supporting both extensions, the deployment module of the "
"custom extension will usually depend on the SPI modules - `quarkus-jaxrs-spi-"
"deployment`, `quarkus-resteasy-common-spi`, `quarkus-resteasy-reactive-spi-"
"deployment`,\n"
"while the runtime modules will have `optional` dependencies on the runtime "
"modules of both RESTEasy flavors."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A couple good examples of how Quarkus uses this strategy to support both "
"RESTEasy flavors in the core repository can be seen [here](https://github."
"com/quarkusio/quarkus/pull/21089) and [here](https://github.com/quarkusio/"
"quarkus/pull/20874)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In general, it should not be needed to have two different versions of the "
"custom extension to support both flavors. Such a choice is only strictly "
"necessary if it is desired for the extension consumers (i.e. Quarkus "
"applications) to not have to select a RESTEasy version themselves."
msgstr ""

msgid "Resource and Provider discovery"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Custom extensions that contain JAX-RS Resources, Providers or REST Client "
"interfaces in their runtime modules and depend on Jandex indexing for\n"
"their discovery (for example because they have an empty `META-INF/beans.xml` "
"file) don't have to perform any additional setup to make\n"
"these discoverable by RESTEasy Reactive."
msgstr ""

msgid "Provider registration via Build Items"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Extensions that register providers via build items use the `io.quarkus."
"resteasy.common.spi.ResteasyJaxrsProviderBuildItem` build item in RESTEasy "
"Classic.\n"
"With RESTEasy Reactive however, extensions need to use specific build items, "
"such as `io.quarkus.resteasy.reactive.spi.MessageBodyWriterBuildItem` and "
"`io.quarkus.resteasy.reactive.spi.MessageBodyWriterBuildItem`."
msgstr ""

msgid "REST Client"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Any code that is run as part of a Quarkus application that used the REST "
"Client, can safely use the Reactive REST Client, as all necessary setup for "
"it has been done at the application's static-init phase."
msgstr ""
